<p>Peru&#x27;s National Elections Board confirmed Sunday that Keiko Fujimori of Fuerza Popular and Roberto Sánchez of Juntos por el Perú will compete in a June 7 presidential runoff after neither candidate won more than half of valid votes in the April 12 first round. Official results put Fujimori first with 2,877,678 votes, or 17.19%, and Sánchez second with 2,015,114 votes, or 12.03%. Rafael López Aliaga finished third with 1,993,905 votes, or 11.91%, after a close contest for second place. The confirmation followed a 33-day count by electoral authorities after delays in setting up polling stations and other logistical problems forced voting to continue at some centers into April 13, especially in Lima. JNE president Roberto Burneo said the electoral authority would correct flaws before the second round and form a five-member supervisory team with experts from Peru, Chile, Uruguay and Puerto Rico, including specialists in cybersecurity and electoral materials.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/BjHoW6hGBlK1fANsIda1FyCY5Aj0L_lgeabh5G1viYFfOIFc6N5p8Mcl3Qs1TWuqOJ7akOS0_a7zDEYxLHCjEVFdb6Jvv8tmp0wBSSh27z_xXDdBfeaR373ntHgue6YI2TYmMsLSDS3qr24gNcNKI6lQs9-lf43wLUgcxvsgu_fumirT1jtzyKLwGT1Bk6Jf7dub9ZnE1EAt8_Z81tyTJtuvvixlSGd04kQj7C4h7PAfBVacxoK5YxAgbPW2iytwAP1jdO4lzg-4PbupQEQSlcYzQewqcKc' alt='Roberto Sánchez and Keiko Fujimori, who will contest Peru&#x27;s presidential runoff.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Narrow margin: Sánchez defeated López Aliaga for the final runoff place by 21,209 votes after the official count.</li><li>Fragmented field: More than 30 candidates competed in the first round, and no candidate exceeded 20% of the vote.</li><li>Post-vote tensions: The New York Times described the period before the runoff as a crisis marked in part by protests.</li><li>Candidate scrutiny: Prosecutors have requested five years and four months in prison for Sánchez over an allegation that he gave false information to electoral authorities about campaign contributions between 2018 and 2020.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>National Elections Board: JNE president Roberto Burneo said the results were already defined, the board had fulfilled its constitutional role, and citizens and parties remained free to file appeals. (<a href='https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/internacional/america-latina/2026/05/17/jurado-nacional-de-elecciones-peruano-reafirma-y-oficializa-segunda-vuelta-entre-fujimori-y-sanchez.shtml'>BioBioChile</a>)</li><li>Renovación Popular: López Aliaga&#x27;s party signaled it would challenge the outcome, citing delays, observed tally sheets and fraud allegations after the first-round vote. (<a href='https://www.biobiochile.cl/noticias/internacional/america-latina/2026/05/17/jurado-nacional-de-elecciones-peruano-reafirma-y-oficializa-segunda-vuelta-entre-fujimori-y-sanchez.shtml'>BioBioChile</a>)</li><li>Keiko Fujimori: Fujimori is presenting herself as the candidate best able to impose order in a country affected by rising crime, extortion and urban violence. (<a href='https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/mundo/2026/05/17/peru-confirma-resultados-de-elecciones-quienes-disputaran-la-segunda-vuelta-presidencial/'>El Financiero</a>)</li><li>Roberto Sánchez: Sánchez gained support in the Andes and Amazon with proposals that include renegotiating natural-resource exploitation contracts. (<a href='https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/mundo/2026/05/17/peru-confirma-resultados-de-elecciones-quienes-disputaran-la-segunda-vuelta-presidencial/'>El Financiero</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/17/world/americas/peru-runoff-election.html'>Runoff in Peru Offers Two Starkly Differing Visions for the Nation</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/05/17/left-wing-candidate-roberto-sanchez-advances-to-presidential-runoff-in-peru_6753533_4.html'>Left-wing candidate Roberto Sanchez advances to presidential runoff in Peru</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li>